{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791037535,"nonce":"oEqZKlencUHHLTLuxWS6jKwUwKQ=","title":"A function over --jit-max-args stays interpreted with no word about it","message":"Reported by the ChimpMonk session (2026-10-03): an 11-argument Float32x4 kernel ran at interpreter speed in hybrid, ~1000x slower than --mode jit, and nothing said why.\n\nThe default cap has been 32 since b3e87e4 (2026-09-30), so at 643fc3c and later that kernel is promoted. On HEAD: ChimpMonk's example/checks.hxml takes 1.24-1.31 s in default hybrid and 1.22 s with --jit-max-args 32, and the tier log shows uniform entries for its 10-, 11-, 13- and 16-argument functions. The published 0.1.0 release predates b3e87e4 and still caps at 8, which is very likely what the report's \"default hybrid 1.71 s\" ran.\n\nWhat remains: a function over the cap is refused at its first call and the reason is printed only under --jit-log. Fix: say it once per process, naming the function, unless --quiet.","files":null}]}